<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bactria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bactrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bactria was a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxus">Amu Darya</a>, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and neighbouring countries. It was a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the Achaemenid and Seleucid empires until its Greek governors broke away to form the Greco-Bactrian kingdom in the third century BCE. It was later affected by nomadic migrations set in motion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bactria.jpg" alt="Bactria" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BactriaMap.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
